<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="wogonin metabolism" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7213" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. However, there is some evidence in the literature that this pathway is specialized to species that do not include Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME:TAX-65409| (Baikal skullcap), a species of the &lt;i&gt;Lamiacea&lt;/i&gt; family, belongs to the 50 most important medicinal herbs used in traditional Chinese medicine. Especially the roots of this plant are rich on flavones which are characteristic tracer compounds in |FRAME:TAX-4139| plants. The flavone aglycones and glucuronide |FRAME:CPD-12725|, |FRAME:CPD-12724| (see |FRAME:PWY-7212|), |FRAME:CPD-12728| and |FRAME:CPD-12727| are the most extensively studied flavones from |FRAME:TAX-4139| and have shown interesting pharmacological properties |CITS: [20708926]|. |FRAME:CPD-12727 Wogonin| has been shown its antitumor properties by inducing apoptosis in malignant cells |CITS:[23371323]|, inhibits the |FRAME:HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| induced vascular permeability which may have therapeutic potential for treating breast cancer |CITS:[23246481]|, showed neuro-protective properties in human neural stem cells |CITS:[23063682]| and has been successfully tested as agent against ocular diseases |CITS:[22928823]|. The metabolism of |FRAME:CPD-12727| comprises 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucurosylation |CITS:[10724177]|, deglucuronidation |CITS:[Morimoto95]|, |CITS:[8593473]|, |CITS: [10858442]| and 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation |CITS:[10872235]| reactions. In contrary to |FRAME:CPD-12724| the 8-methylated flavone |FRAME:CPD-12727| is not an accepted substrate for cell wall peroxidases and therefore not involved in the detoxification of |FRAME:HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| (see |FRAME:PWY-7214|).</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
    <Author name="O-28" />
    <Author name="hartmut" />
  </Authors>
